              {
                "text": "I'm trying to figure out how the two 5-markers define a bounding region, and how each shape inside gets expanded into a completed rectangle based on its original position and size.\n\nLooking at example 2, shapes below the marker line become squares while shapes above seem to get erased and replaced with a large filled block of 5s spanning multiple rows and columns.\n\nIn example 3 both regions above and below the markers end up filled entirely with 5, so I'm now wondering if it's specifically diagonal shapes that trigger this full-fill behavior versus simple shapes that just get squared off.\n\nTesting a new theory: the filled region's bounding box seems to match the union of all shapes within that region\u2014the 7 and 1 shapes above the marker combine into a bounding box of rows0-2, cols2-9, and that's exactly what gets filled with 5s. I'm now checking whether the below-region in example 3 follows the same union-bounding-box logic with the 3 and 6 shapes.\n\nI think the distinguishing factor is whether the 5s split the grid into aligned halves: when they do, multiple shapes within a half merge into one 5-filled bounding box, but a single shape in a half completes into a rectangle of its own color. In ex1 the 5s sit at corners without dividing the grid, so each shape completes individually in its own color instead of merging.\n\n",
